@h1 Inline marks h1@

Beyond the two shorthand marks for _italic_ and *bold*, Wit gives you a small
family of inline elements you call by name: code, underline, strike-through,
subscript and superscript, a highlighter, small print, a work title, and a hard
line break. Each is a node — you open it with a name and close it with the same
name — and each maps straight to the matching HTML element.

@h2 Why call them by name h2@

The two mark characters cover the emphasis you reach for constantly. Everything
else is deliberately spelled out, so your prose stays clean and the intent is
obvious. You use these when you need a semantic element that no two-character
mark provides.

@h2 The basic shape h2@

Inline marks are inline nodes: they wrap a run of text and flatten into a single
element, with no stray paragraph inside. Inline code is the one you will reach
for most:

@@@pre
Call @code lighthouse.foo() code@ to relight the lamp.
pre@@@

renders `Call` followed by @code lighthouse.foo() code@ as inline code, then the
rest of the sentence.

@h2 The full set h2@

Every one of these is written the same way — @@@code @name … name@ code@@@ — and
produces the same-named HTML tag:

@@@pre
@code … code@    → <code>    inline code: commands, identifiers, paths
@u … u@       → <u>       underline (reads as a link online — use sparingly)
@s … s@       → <s>       strike-through: retractions, "was X, now Y"
@sub … sub@     → <sub>     subscript: chemical formulae, footnote marks
@sup … sup@     → <sup>     superscript: exponents, ordinals, note refs
@mark … mark@    → <mark>    highlight: draw the eye to a phrase
@small … small@   → <small>   small print: asides, fine print
@cite … cite@    → <cite>    the title of a referenced work
@br br@        → <br>      a hard line break inside one paragraph
pre@@@

Here they are in a single run:

@@@pre
The result @u underlined u@, an @s edit s@, water is H@sub 2 sub@O,
the area is 4@sup 2 sup@, a @mark highlighted mark@ phrase,
some @small small print small@, and a work like @cite Moby-Dick cite@.
pre@@@

@h2 The hard line break h2@

A lone newline in your source is a soft wrap that reads as a space. When you want
a _real_ line break inside a single paragraph — an address block, a line of verse,
a signature — use the self-closing break node:

@@@pre
Ada Lovelace @br br@
12 Analytical Way @br br@
London
pre@@@

Each break node is written @@@code @br br@ code@@@ and needs no body. In Markdown
output a line break degrades to a space, since Markdown has no reliable inline
break — so if hard breaks matter, keep to HTML or PDF output.

@h2 They flatten to one element h2@

Every inline mark is _inline-context_: it wraps its text directly, so
@@@code @em word em@ code@@@ becomes @code <em>word</em> code@, never
@code <em><p>word</p></em> code@. That is what lets you drop them mid-sentence
without breaking the flow of a paragraph.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Forgetting the closing name._ An inline node you open but never close raises
an "unclosed node" error. Every @@@code @code … code@ code@@@ needs its
@@@code code@ code@@@. li@
@li _Using inline code to hold an at-sign._ An inline code node that contains an
email breaks, because the @@@code keeper@ code@@@ inside it is read as a close.
In prose, escape the at-sign — @@@code keeper\@example.org code@@@ — or use a
frozen inline literal. See @a |href /docs/write/prose/| Paragraphs & prose a@. li@
@li _Reaching for underline for emphasis._ Online, underline reads as a link. Use
_italic_ or *bold* for stress and keep underline for the rare case that truly
means it. li@
ul@

